A `canvasImage` reaches every target's draw function.

It was the last shape still translated by name on html -- six of the seven had
a platform override and this one did not, because the drawing is asynchronous
and neither `new Image()` nor a load listener is something a native mark can
spell. So html declares a shim on the page and the override calls it, the same
trade android makes for its own image and text.

Nothing in testdata drew an image at all, which is how the gap survived: the
platform harnesses compile every fixture, and none of them named this shape.

The shim carries a decode cache, which the name-matched translation did not: a
canvas redraws on every state change and that path built a fresh `Image` each
time, so a drawing with an image in it re-entered the network stack per frame
and blinked as each decode landed. It is written only for a page that draws
one -- the JS translator records the natives a call reached, and html asks.

deny out/none/html/index.html `\bCanvasDrawImage\(` -- html renders the image through its override, not the name-matched intrinsic
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `\bCanvasDrawImage\(` -- and Go renders it through the language override

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import draw "sngl:ui/draw"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

component main() ui.node {
    draw.canvas(width=40px, height=20px) {
        draw.canvasImage(x=1.0, y=2.0, w=8.0, h=4.0, src="logo.png")
    }
}

ui.window {
    main()
}
-- out/go/bubbletea/model.go --
